00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.225
So Amitabh, what
exactly is a cloud PC?

00:00:48.225 --> 00:00:50.100
AMITABH SINHA: That's
a great question, Brad.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.850
A cloud PC is very similar
to your physical PC.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.000
It runs Windows 10, it
runs your applications.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.540
The big difference is, a
cloud PC runs in the cloud,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.100
in the Google Cloud.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.900
You get the same
excellent performance

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.970
as your physical PC.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.880
There are key
differences, though.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.170
A cloud PC, you get
data center class

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.780
security versus a
physical PC, you're

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.290
worried about endpoint security.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:20.430
A physical PC can take four
to six weeks to provision,

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.490
a cloud PC is available
instantly anywhere

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:24.660
in the world.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.760
A physical PC has
a refresh cycle

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.130
every three to four
years, a cloud PC

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.530
is instantly reconfigurable.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.780
You can change the CPU,
the memory, the storage,

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.490
anytime you want.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.560
Our customers are
using cloud PCs

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.300
to enable work
from anywhere, they

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.860
are enabling secure remote
access from any device,

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.370
contractors are using it,
call center employees,

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:51.990
remote software developers,
and a very high performance use

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.670
case is 3D collaboration.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.550
So for 3D CAD engineers
in architecture

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.140
ending in construction
and manufacturing,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.850
auto manufacturing.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.290
These are very, very
special use cases.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.390
What we had to do for
cloud PCs in order

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.110
to deliver this innovation
was to start from scratch

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:12.200
and build a cloud-native
architecture, which

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.840
very closely resembles something
that Google has called Anthos.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.860
Anthos is a fantastic
platform for you

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.850
to deploy and manage containers
on-prem in multiple clouds

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.170
and the edge.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.870
And the port solution
is just like Anthos,

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.332
except for Windows 10 cloud PCs.

00:02:30.332 --> 00:02:31.790
BRAD PETERSON: Well
Amitabh, that's

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.420
a great way to sum that up, the
difference in how we deliver

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.440
low-latency cloud
PCs essentially

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.380
is because we can do
it all around the world

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.820
and put it in the cloud
region next to the user.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.880
So it's low-latency,
it's right next to them.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.160
But also there's that
fundamental architecture

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.410
that's massively different,
and I love the fact

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:50.440
that Anthos is so
similar to the Workspot

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:51.730
architecture in that way.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.370
Only for us, of course,
it's Windows 10 desktops.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.380
Historically, this
was not the case.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:59.300
Historically, what had
happened is legacy systems

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.930
were out there with VDI,
Virtual Desktop Infrastructure.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.570
Now with this it
was very different.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.895
The IT organization had
to buy everything upfront

00:03:06.895 --> 00:03:08.770
and they were responsible
for building it all

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:12.343
and making it work, and this was
only in one location at a time.

00:03:12.343 --> 00:03:14.260
And so the problem is
the end users were never

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.760
really happy with
the performance,

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.560
because it's like they were
sharing resources all the time.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.530
It might be OK in the
morning, but not so OK

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.630
in the afternoon when
everyone came in.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.075
And also if they were far
away from that one location

00:03:25.075 --> 00:03:27.450
in the world, they were getting
high latency connections,

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.080
and that was super slow.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.840
The IT organization wasn't happy
either with the notion of this

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.850
do it yourself
project, because they

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:37.240
had full ownership of everything
and huge upfront costs,

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.970
CAPEX costs, and also the
notion of the complexity

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.430
and the responsibility
of making it all work.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.370
Now that's kind of bad news, but
you know what the good news is?

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.370
You can forget everything you
know about VDI, because that's

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:51.340
not required in this
new world of ours.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:52.900
Significantly different.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.690
If you are an IT person that has
suffered through this before,

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:57.080
you know what I'm talking about.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.070
And even if you're an end user,
and you've requested a VDI

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.060
desktop and it has been
painfully slow and so forth,

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.060
you've experienced that.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.602
You also know what
I'm talking about.

00:04:05.602 --> 00:04:09.100
Forget that whole world,
everything changes now.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.770
And it changes now because
of the title of this session,

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:13.850
Low-Latency Cloud PC.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:17.290
Low-latency because the
desktop is in the cloud,

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.483
region of the world is close
to the user as possible.

00:04:20.483 --> 00:04:22.150
And they're getting
their own resources,

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:23.525
they're not sharing
these things,

00:04:23.525 --> 00:04:26.380
they're for them so it's
super fast and wonderful.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.080
And on the IT side
it's a cloud PC,

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.110
so you're not responsible for
taking it, buying it, building

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:33.340
it, and then maintaining it.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.320
It's a subscription service.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.340
You subscribe to 100 desktops or
200 desktops or 1,000 desktops

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.720
in New York, LA,
London or Singapore,

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.460
or wherever you want
around the world.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:44.960
And even though
it's that simple,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.690
it's also enterprise-class.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:50.080
It's tied into your
enterprise and your AD

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.350
authentication and your Windows
10 images and so on and so on.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:56.500
It's also massively predictable
in the world of cost

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.120
because it's flat-rate pricing.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.830
No matter where you need that
desktop anywhere in the world,

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:03.820
it is the exact
same flat-rate price

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.790
across the world, any location,
any user, and any use case.

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:09.640
So that's the amazing part.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:10.670
What does it look like?

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:13.910
Well, if I'm sitting at my
desk, maybe at home here,

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:16.870
I might have a Chromebook
or a smartphone or a tablet.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:19.630
And if I install the
Workspot client on there,

