00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.710
And so it's just
unbelievably amazing.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.390
Now what we're going
to do is drill down

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.490
into those two words
we started off with,

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.440
a low-latency words,
the cloud PC words,

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.000
we're going to dive
into low-latency

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.310
and go into more detail on
how that's truly the case.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.787
So for this, Amitabh, why
don't you take this section?

00:08:11.787 --> 00:08:12.620
AMITABH SINHA: Yeah.

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:13.610
BRAD PETERSON: Low-latency.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:14.777
AMITABH SINHA: Thanks, Brad.

00:08:14.777 --> 00:08:17.120
Low-latency is driven by
a controlled data plane

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.080
separation and architecture.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.870
And this is the most
fundamental thing

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:24.530
that one needs to think
about in the cloud PC world.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:28.080
Low-latency drives excellent
performance for end users.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.250
So let's try to explain to
you how all this happens,

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:34.820
and we start with understanding
first the problems with VDI.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.000
So if you look at how
VDI was deployed before,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.929
this was in an era when most
customers had one data center.

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:44.870
The largest ones might have
two, three data centers.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.690
But because of the cost
complexity of the solutions

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:51.110
stack, you deployed VDI
in a single data center.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:54.860
And in that single data
center the control plane,

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.920
which used to configure
provision and broker

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.810
virtual desktops, and
the data plane, which

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:02.180
is the user's
desktops themselves,

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:06.120
were all very tightly integrated
into the same data center.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.300
And so your users could
now be 50, 100, 150, 200,

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.893
250 milliseconds away
from that data center.

00:09:11.893 --> 00:09:14.060
And the further away they
were from the data center,

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:16.180
the worse the performance was.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:19.190
That whole industry
spent the last 10 years

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:22.820
trying to optimize and
mitigate the problems

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:24.860
with this low latency
by deploying [? van ?]

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:28.070
acceleration technology
or protocol improvements.

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:30.420
But fundamentally, it's
the speed of light.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:31.370
It's a law of physics.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:35.690
You can only do so much with
optimizing the latencies that

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:38.420
are created by VDI.

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.060
Now the world is
changing completely.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:45.290
We're entering an era of
low-latency infrastructure.

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:48.560
So on the left, you start it
off with on-prem data centers

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.480
where the latencies
can be low if you're

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:53.330
next to the data centers, but
very high if you're far away.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:56.570
And most customers had access to
one, maybe two or three regions

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:57.940
in the world.

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:00.620
Today with the public
cloud, with Google Cloud,

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:03.050
every customer has
access to tens and dozens

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:04.760
of regions across the world.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:09.560
And they're now within 15 to 20
milliseconds of a Google Cloud

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:11.970
region anywhere they
are in the world.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.532
And that's new, that
wasn't possible before.

00:10:14.532 --> 00:10:16.490
And going forward it's
going to get even better

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.880
from a latency perspective,
because all the public clouds

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:23.750
are working with carriers to
deploy edge and 5G technology

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:25.580
so that this high
compute power is going

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:29.120
to be available in thousands of
regions across the world, where

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:30.590
the user is going
to be within five

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:35.330
milliseconds latency of any of
these infrastructure pieces.

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:36.770
It's not
straightforward to start

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:39.312
with the world on the left and
move to the world on the right

00:10:39.312 --> 00:10:40.700
with any piece of software.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:42.770
And this is not
just VDI software.

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:46.610
When we built databases back in
2005, built app servers or even

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.770
SAP, it was all
built the same way.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.530
It was built for a
single data center.

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.380
Some vendors might choose
to make copies of it

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.530
across the world, but how
many copies are you going

00:10:56.530 --> 00:10:57.740
to make for the edge world?

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:00.440
How many copies are you going to
make in the public cloud world?

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.020
That's not the
right architecture.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:04.880
And the folks who
inspired us were

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:07.040
from software-defined
networking,

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.440
and software-defined network
had to solve this problem

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.210
before anybody else.

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:14.270
And what they implemented
was a new architecture,

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:17.270
which is control data
plane separation.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:20.940
And we start off with wireless
access points as an example.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:24.140
Meraki was one of the
early vendors in this space

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:26.930
and they had this
problem to be solved,

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:29.580
because every customer
had multiple buildings

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.340
and offices around the world.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:33.200
Each office could
have multiple floors,

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:35.690
each floor could
have multiple areas,

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:38.090
and you couldn't go around
installing access points

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:40.580
and control planes and data
planes all over the world.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.450
It just doesn't make any sense.

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:44.090
And so they created
a control plane

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.280
that was separate from
the data plate, which

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:47.660
is the access point.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.030
And you could deploy the data
planes anywhere you want,

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:52.340
and the control
plane allowed you to,

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.890
from a single pane of glass,
to configure all these data

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:56.970
planes all over the world.

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:59.120
Now we took
inspiration from this

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.760
and built a very
similar architecture.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:05.150
But instead of access points,
it's Windows 10 cloud PCs.

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:08.280
And they could be distributed
anywhere in the world.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:12.560
So our control plane is very
separate from our data plane.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.740
From [? our ?] control
plane, any customer of ours

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:19.550
can deploy Windows 10 cloud
PCs into any Google Cloud

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:23.180
region that exists today
or will ever exist.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:27.140
And in the future we will also
support all the edge regions.

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:29.420
Now this separation of data
plane and control plane

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:34.920
is very, very important for
cloud PCs for multiple reasons.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:38.595
So we separated out per
customer, per region.

00:12:38.595 --> 00:12:40.720
And this is very important
from first a performance

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.720
perspective, because
not every user is

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:47.160
within 50 millisecond latency
of a Google Cloud region.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.150
They are on a local
high speed internet

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:51.040
and they get
fantastic bandwidth,

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:53.150
and so they get
excellent performance.

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:55.780
It's also better from a
scalability perspective,

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:59.200
because we can actually scale
each region independently

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:00.580
for each customer.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:03.130
It's better from an
availability perspective.

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:05.945
If a region goes down,
only one customer--

00:13:05.945 --> 00:13:08.320
the customers in that region
and the users in that region

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:09.790
are affected.

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:11.860
And it's better from a
security perspective,

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:14.360
because we don't commingle
data from multiple customers.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.550
They have separate data planes.

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:19.310
With Google Cloud, another
benefit that we get

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:22.430
is we get the benefits
of all the optimizations

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:26.360
that Google Cloud has done
for YouTube as a workload.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:28.400
YouTube is a real-time
video workload

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.080
that billions of
people are watching

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:33.050
in the world every
day, and optimizations

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:35.270
that Google has made
for that workload

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:39.220
applies very, very closely to
the workload we're delivering.

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:41.600
And so this separation
leads to low latency which

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:43.980
leads to excellent performance.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:45.480
And so if you talk
to our customers,

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:49.340
the biggest thing they'll talk
about versus the VDI use case

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:53.180
was our customers and our users
love their Workspot cloud PC.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:56.180
We have customers like
Southland replacing

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:59.720
expensive $10,000 liquid
cool workstations for 3D CAD

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:03.020
workstations with Workspot
cloud workstations.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:06.370
We have Mead &amp; Hunt, which has
been measuring productivity

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.990
of their employees from
home and from office,

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.660
and have measured it and
said it's equal, if not

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:13.580
slightly better from home.

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:17.030
And so our users really love
the performance of the cloud PC.

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:19.790
And this is unlike what
I'm sure most of you

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:21.500
have heard from your
VDI users, where

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:25.700
people are unhappy with
the performance of VDI.

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:29.510
Now let's have Brad talk about
how what we have done really

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:31.050
makes life simple for IT.

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.230
So you have made
end users happy,

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:36.322
but we've also put in a lot
of effort to make IT happy.

00:14:36.322 --> 00:14:37.280
BRAD PETERSON: Exactly.

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:38.072
Thanks, Amitabh.

00:14:38.072 --> 00:14:39.530
And with that,
we're going to focus

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:41.420
on the cloud PC side over here.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:44.390
Again, talk about the fact
that the new architecture

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:47.150
is this SaaS offering, a
subscription offering, where

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:49.370
you just ask for
the desktop that you

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:51.388
want in that part of the world.

00:14:51.388 --> 00:14:53.930
And so before we do that, we're
going to go backwards in time

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:54.472
a little bit.

00:14:54.472 --> 00:14:56.170
How did this used to be before?

00:14:56.170 --> 00:14:57.920
When you take a look
at this, and you look

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:00.110
at this VDI solutions stack--

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:02.773
Virtual Desktop Infrastructure--
again, in this world

00:15:02.773 --> 00:15:04.190
here if you've
ever done this, you

00:15:04.190 --> 00:15:05.840
pick a location in
the world and you

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:09.110
purchase all sorts of
software and hardware,

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:11.410
and a truck comes and dumps
it all on your driveway.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:13.160
And you pull it all
into your data center,

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:14.940
and you have to build
all this from scratch.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:16.440
You pay for the
whole thing upfront.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:17.518
It's all your risk.

00:15:17.518 --> 00:15:19.560
And think about this,
you've got to build it out.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:23.270
So you've got CPU,
network, memory, storage,

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:25.700
all this utilization go
ins to-- their capacity

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:28.580
is set to support a certain
number of virtual desktops

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:29.260
and so forth.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:31.760
And then you've got to put a
brokering system on that so you

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:34.690
can get your users to come in
and connect to the right space,

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.440
but then you have all
sorts of integration

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:38.870
you have to do into maybe
authentication or back end

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:39.770
systems.

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:42.140
And then you've got
various layers of support.

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:45.410
You got support layers just to
support the stack you built,

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:48.200
and then other support layers to
actually support the end users

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:49.170
and so forth.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:50.480
So you got it, right?

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:53.990
So it's complicated, expensive,
and you are completely

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:57.380
on the hook, and that was just
one location in the world.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:00.040
So how does that contrast
with this new architecture

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:01.340
from Workspot?

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:03.968
The idea now is you don't
buy any of that up front,

00:16:03.968 --> 00:16:05.510
and you don't build
any of that ever,

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:07.460
and you don't support it either.

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:09.350
When you actually
want a desktop that's

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:11.510
configured with a certain
number of GPU memory

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.282
and storage and a
location of the world,

00:16:13.282 --> 00:16:14.990
you sort of click
that, push that button,

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:16.010
and it is provisioned.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:18.530
And that person can
come in in minutes

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.490
and consume that
and start working.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:23.120
In fact, in the upcoming demo,
we're going to show you that.

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:26.060
We're going to launch
thousands of new desktops

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:28.820
in multiple regions around the
world in this demonstration

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:30.350
right here in the
next few minutes,

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:32.767
and it's only going to take a
